{#
  Case study summary card — a discovery tile that links to a full case study page.
  Media on top, then the client (eyebrow), the case study title, an optional blurb,
  and a row of skill chips. The whole card is the link.

  Params (only url + title are required; the rest degrade gracefully):
    url     — the case study page URL
    title   — the case study title (heading)
    client  — the client it was for (small eyebrow above the title)
    summary — a short public blurb
    skills  — list of skill labels (chips)
    image   — Asset for the media (cream placeholder shows until one exists)
    loading — 'eager' (default) | 'lazy' for a card in normal document flow
    restTitle — true to show the title WITHOUT a hover (the feature layout). It moves
                the h3 out of the collapsing box rather than revealing it in place:
                that box animates 0fr -> 1fr and clips, so nothing inside it can be
                visible at rest. Summary and skills stay where they are.
#}
{% import '_macros/media' as media %}
{% set client = client ?? '' %}
{% set summary = summary ?? '' %}
{% set skills = skills ?? [] %}
{% set image = image ?? null %}
{# Included with `only`, so every optional var needs a default here or Twig throws
   on the ones a caller omits — the marquee passes no logo, and without this the
   whole panel failed to render (silently, since renderComponent swallows it). #}
{% set logo = logo ?? null %}
{% set restTitle = restTitle ?? false %}
{# Eager by DEFAULT because of where this card is usually met: inside a Jonson answer,
   in a panel composed off-screen and revealed as one piece. A lazy image there never
   intersects a viewport while it's being built, so it never loads and the card arrives
   empty. Callers that put the card in normal document flow — the static strip at the
   foot of a case study — pass 'lazy' and get the browser's own behaviour back. #}
{% set loading = loading ?? 'eager' %}

{# Thumbnail widths. The media well is 3:2 and the transforms are width-only, so the
   source's own ratio is preserved and the CSS `object-fit: cover` does any cropping —
   an upload at the field's stated 2220x1480 needs neither.

   Two layouts, two sets of widths, because their display sizes differ by 2x:
     marquee — slide is (1110 - gap) / 2 = 547, so 547 / 1094 covers 1x and 2x
     feature — card spans the 1110 panel, so 1110 / 2220 does

   The caller passes `imageWidths` + `imageSizes` together; the defaults here are the
   marquee's, since that's the variant the strip and Jonson's [[casestudies:all]] use.
   Below md both layouts go full width, which the trailing `100vw` covers. #}
{% set imageWidths = imageWidths ?? [547, 1094] %}
{% set imageSizes = imageSizes ?? '(min-width: 48em) 547px, 100vw' %}
{% set imageFallback = imageFallback ?? 547 %}

<a class="c-case-study" href="{{ url }}">
    <div class="c-case-study__media">
        {% if image %}
            {# Quality raised — the thumbnail IS the card, and banding in a flat brand
               background is exactly what 82 gives you. `loading` is the caller's
               (see the default above).

               `tone: true` — the card arrives on a wash of its own two colours
               rather than the well's flat placeholder. On here for BOTH variants:
               the feature card is one big picture and the marquee is a row of them,
               and either way the card is the thing being looked at, not a thumbnail
               beside text (which is the case the macro keeps it off for). #}
            {{ media.picture(image, imageSizes, {
                class: 'c-case-study__img',
                widths: imageWidths,
                fallback: imageFallback,
                loading: loading,
                quality: 90,
                tone: true,
            }) }}
        {% endif %}
    </div>
    {# Logo sits left of the text when the client has one in the Client List
       (matched by name in FindContext::clientLogo). Most don't, so the row has to
       read properly with the chip absent — hence __text carrying its own column
       rather than the logo being positioned against the meta box. #}
    <div class="c-case-study__meta">
        {% if logo %}
            {# Vector — serve as-is, same as the clients marquee. No transform, no
               webp: it's crisp at any size and a raster pipeline buys nothing.
               Eager, because the panel is revealed as a piece and a lazy image
               inside it never intersects. #}
            <span class="c-case-study__logo">
                <img
                    class="c-case-study__logo-img"
                    src="{{ logo.url }}"
                    alt="{{ client ?: title }}"
                    height="64"
                    decoding="async">
            </span>
        {% endif %}
        {# The client sits outside the collapsing box, so it's the only line with any
           height before a hover — which is what drops it to the foot of the card,
           onto the scrim, rather than leaving it stranded halfway up above three
           invisible-but-still-tall siblings.

           __reveal / __reveal-inner are a pair on purpose, not one box with a class
           to spare: collapsing to nothing and back needs a grid row that animates
           (0fr → 1fr) AND a child that clips while it does. One element can't be
           both. See _case-study.scss. #}
        <div class="c-case-study__text">
            {% if not restTitle %}
                {# Marquee. The arrow is the card's one resting mark, so this row renders
                   whether or not there is a client to put in it — gated on `client` it
                   meant a study with no client on file got no arrow at all, and nothing
                   said the card was a link. The arrow is positioned against the card
                   rather than this row (see _case-study.scss), so an empty row costs
                   nothing and places it identically. #}
                <div class="c-case-study__head">
                    {% if client %}<p class="c-case-study__client">{{ client }}</p>{% endif %}
                    <svg class="c-case-study__go" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                        <path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                    </svg>
                </div>
            {% elseif client %}
                <p class="c-case-study__client">{{ client }}</p>
            {% endif %}
            {% if restTitle %}
                {# Title and arrow are one row so the arrow can sit on the title's FIRST
                   line — it used to be pinned to the card's top right, which put it a
                   long way from the thing it acts on. In the row it is pushed to the
                   right edge by margin-left:auto and aligned to the top, so a title that
                   wraps to two lines leaves it on line one. #}
                <div class="c-case-study__head">
                    <h3 class="c-case-study__title">{{ title }}</h3>
                    <svg class="c-case-study__go" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                        <path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                    </svg>
                </div>
            {% endif %}
            <div class="c-case-study__reveal">
                <div class="c-case-study__reveal-inner">
                    {% if not restTitle %}<h3 class="c-case-study__title">{{ title }}</h3>{% endif %}
                    {# Feature cards show logo, client and title at rest, which left this
                       collapsing box with nothing in it — an empty 0fr -> 1fr animation
                       and a hover that revealed nothing. This is what it reveals now.
                       NOT an <a>: the whole card is already the link, and a link inside
                       a link is invalid and unreachable. #}

                    {% if summary %}<p class="c-case-study__summary">{{ summary }}</p>{% endif %}
                    {% if skills is not empty %}
                        <ul class="c-case-study__skills">
                            {% for skill in skills %}
                                <li class="c-case-study__skill">{{ skill }}</li>
                            {% endfor %}
                        </ul>
                    {% endif %}
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</a>
